{
  "id": "reorder-list",
  "questionId": "reorder-list",
  "slug": "reorder-list",
  "title": "Reorder List",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Fast slow pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "fast & slow pointers",
    "linked list",
    "reversal"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "you need to pick alternately from the front and the back of the list - what's awkward about walking a singly linked list backward?",
    "what if you split the list into two halves and reversed the second half, so both halves could now be walked forward?",
    "use fast/slow pointers to find the middle and split the list there, then reverse the second half in place",
    "merge the two halves by alternating one node from the first half, then one from the (reversed) second half, until you run out of nodes"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "getting the split point wrong on odd-length lists, causing the middle element to be duplicated or dropped",
    "forgetting to terminate the first half's tail before reversing the second half, which can cause an infinite loop if using real pointers",
    "merging in the wrong order (e.g. second half node before first half node)"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A singly linked list is given to you as an array `nums`, where `nums[i]`\nis the value of the i-th node in order: [n0, n1, n2, ..., nk].\n\n**Reorder it to interleave from both ends, producing [n0, nk, n1, nk-1, n2, nk-2, ...], and return the resulting list's values in order.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 5 * 10^4",
      "-1000 <= nums[i] <= 1000"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node list",
      "a two-node list",
      "odd-length list (middle element stays put) vs even-length"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]",
        "output": "[1, 4, 2, 3]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 4, 2, 3]",
        "explanation": "n0=1, nk=4, n1=2, nk-1=3, interleaved as 1,4,2,3"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]",
        "output": "[1, 5, 2, 4, 3]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 5, 2, 4, 3]",
        "explanation": "interleave from both ends: 1,5,2,4, and the middle 3 left in place"
